From 3f974cfa7ada8cfff21c74356497901e9baf8830 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djnice <djnice@freemail.hu>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 06:36:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audiotrack: no delay reported for the "native" device's
 direct track
Message-Id: <20261111-dvfel-audiotrack-native-nodelay@djnice>

Music on the "native" device (0035: a direct output at the track's own
rate, Dune HD boxes) stalled in its first second, and again after every
seek: the empty direct AudioTrack, played at the start and after a flush,
was paused by AudioFlinger for its underrun ("pause because of UNDERRUN,
framesReady = 0"), the HAL went to standby and back, and the timestamps
that followed made the samples look 100-160 ms early: the aout inserted
that much silence ("playback way too early: playing silence"). The usual
mixed output was not affected (a mild down-sampling only). Playing the
track only once it had 125 / 250 ms of frames did not help.

The native rate's track is music only (the player gives no picture to
keep to): TimeGet reports no delay for it, as for the passthrough
encodings without one; the aout then inserts nothing, the AudioTrack's
buffer paces the writes.
---
 modules/audio_output/audiotrack.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/modules/audio_output/audiotrack.c b/modules/audio_output/audiotrack.c
index 2310a58b5d..8d77bf2bf5 100644
--- a/modules/audio_output/audiotrack.c
+++ b/modules/audio_output/audiotrack.c
@@ -918,6 +918,15 @@ TimeGet( audio_output_t *p_aout, vlc_tick_t *restrict p_delay )
                                || p_sys->fmt.i_format != VLC_CODEC_SPDIFL ) )
         return -1;
 
+    /* The native rate's direct track (AT_DEV_NATIVE, music: no picture to
+     * keep to) neither: AudioFlinger paused it for its underrun at every start
+     * and seek, the HAL went to standby and back, and its timestamps made the
+     * samples look 100-160 ms early - the aout inserted that much silence,
+     * heard as a stall in the first second. Without a delay nothing is
+     * inserted; the AudioTrack's buffer paces the writes. */
+    if( p_sys->b_native_rate )
+        return -1;
+
     vlc_mutex_lock( &p_sys->lock );
 
     if( p_sys->b_error || !p_sys->i_samples_written || !( env = GET_ENV() ) )
-- 
